CAT = "cblContract"; SEG = "CON"
Q = [
("Under common law, an offeree's silence in response to an offer is generally:",
 "Not an acceptance", ["An acceptance", "A counteroffer", "A binding option"],
 "Acceptance normally has to be communicated, so silence does not create a contract unless the parties' past dealings or an agreement make silence a way to accept."),
("A court enforces a promise without consideration because the other side reasonably relied on it. This is:",
 "Promissory estoppel", ["Quantum meruit", "Novation", "Accord and satisfaction"],
 "Promissory estoppel enforces a promise when the promisor should expect reliance, the other party did rely, and injustice can be avoided only by enforcement."),
("Recovering the reasonable value of work done when no enforceable contract covers it is called:",
 "Quantum meruit", ["Promissory estoppel", "Specific performance", "Nominal damages"],
 "Quantum meruit, meaning as much as deserved, lets a party recover the fair value of work the other party accepted, which keeps the other side from being unjustly enriched."),
("Under the UCC, a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more generally must be:",
 "In a signed writing", ["Notarized by both", "Recorded with the county", "Witnessed by two people"],
 "UCC section 2-201, the statute of frauds for goods, requires a writing signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought for sales of $500 or more, with some exceptions."),
("A breach that defeats the main purpose of the contract is a:",
 "Material breach", ["Minor breach", "Technical default", "Constructive change"],
 "A material breach goes to the heart of the bargain and lets the other party stop performing and seek damages. A minor breach allows damages but not termination."),
("Before performance is due, one party states it will not perform. This is:",
 "Anticipatory repudiation", ["Mutual rescission", "Novation", "Accord and satisfaction"],
 "Anticipatory repudiation is a clear refusal to perform made before the due date, and the other party may treat it as a breach right away."),
("A preset daily amount a contractor owes the owner for finishing late is:",
 "Liquidated damages", ["Punitive damages", "Retainage", "Consequential damages"],
 "Liquidated damages are agreed in the contract as a reasonable estimate of the owner's loss from delay. Courts may refuse to enforce an amount that acts as a penalty."),
("Profits an owner loses because a building opens late are an example of:",
 "Consequential damages", ["Direct damages", "Nominal damages", "Restitution damages"],
 "Consequential damages flow indirectly from the breach, such as lost rent or profits. Many construction contracts include a mutual waiver of them."),
("A clause making one party cover the other's losses from third-party claims is:",
 "Indemnification", ["Arbitration", "Force majeure", "Waiver of lien"],
 "An indemnity clause shifts responsibility for certain losses and claims to one party. How far it can go, such as covering the other party's own negligence, varies by state."),
("Which clause excuses delay caused by events beyond either party's control?",
 "Force majeure", ["Indemnity", "Pay-when-paid", "Flow-down"],
 "A force majeure clause excuses or extends performance for listed events like natural disasters or war. Most give time but not extra money."),
("A subcontract term binding the sub to the prime contract's duties toward the owner is a:",
 "Flow-down clause", ["Pay-if-paid clause", "Escalation clause", "Waiver of subrogation"],
 "A flow-down clause passes the prime contract's obligations, such as specifications and dispute rules, down to the subcontractor."),
("Under a pay-if-paid clause, a subcontractor gets paid only if:",
 "The owner pays the prime contractor", ["The work passes final inspection", "The sub posts a payment bond", "The architect signs the punch list"],
 "Pay-if-paid makes owner payment a condition of paying the sub. Some states refuse to enforce it or read it as pay-when-paid, so the effect varies by state."),
("A process where a neutral person issues a usually binding decision is:",
 "Arbitration", ["Mediation", "Negotiation", "Partnering"],
 "In arbitration the parties present their case to an arbitrator whose award is usually final. Mediation produces no decision unless the parties agree."),
("In mediation, the neutral person:",
 "Helps the parties settle", ["Issues a binding award", "Rules on the evidence", "Sets liquidated damages"],
 "A mediator guides negotiation but cannot impose a result. Issuing a binding award is what an arbitrator does."),
("A contractor hits rock not shown in the soil reports. Which clause usually applies?",
 "Differing site conditions", ["Liquidated damages", "Indemnification", "Termination for convenience"],
 "A differing site conditions clause adjusts price and time when subsurface conditions differ materially from those indicated in the contract or normally expected."),
("Which clause lets an owner end a contract when the contractor is not at fault?",
 "Termination for convenience", ["Termination for cause", "Force majeure", "Time is of the essence"],
 "Termination for convenience lets the owner stop the work without default, usually paying for work done and some costs. Termination for cause requires a contractor default."),
("A warranty the law reads into a contract without it being stated is:",
 "Implied warranty", ["Express warranty", "Manufacturer's warranty", "Extended warranty"],
 "Implied warranties, such as merchantability for goods, arise by law. Express warranties are promises the seller actually states."),
("A reply to an offer that changes its terms is legally a:",
 "Counteroffer", ["Acceptance", "Option contract", "Novation"],
 "A counteroffer rejects the original offer and proposes new terms. Acceptance must match the offer for a contract to form under common law."),
("The rule limiting earlier oral deals from changing a final written contract is the:",
 "Parol evidence rule", ["Statute of frauds", "Mailbox rule", "Statute of limitations"],
 "The parol evidence rule bars prior or contemporaneous agreements from contradicting a final, integrated written contract. The statute of frauds decides which contracts need a writing."),
("The deadline to file a lawsuit after a breach of contract is set by the:",
 "Statute of limitations", ["Parol evidence rule", "Notice to proceed", "Statute of frauds"],
 "A statute of limitations limits how long after a claim arises a suit may be filed. The length varies by state and by type of claim."),
("When contract documents conflict, which clause decides which document controls?",
 "Order of precedence", ["Severability", "Indemnity", "Price escalation"],
 "An order of precedence clause ranks the documents, for example the agreement over the specifications and the specifications over the drawings."),
("Replacing a party to a contract, with all parties agreeing and the old party released, is:",
 "Novation", ["Assignment", "Rescission", "Ratification"],
 "A novation substitutes a new party and releases the old one. An assignment transfers rights, but the original party usually stays liable."),
("Which clause keeps the rest of a contract in force if a court strikes one term?",
 "Severability", ["Integration", "Force majeure", "Flow-down"],
 "A severability clause lets the valid parts of a contract survive when one provision is unenforceable. An integration clause states that the writing is the whole agreement."),
("A clause stating that meeting the deadlines is a material term is a:",
 "Time is of the essence", ["No damage for delay", "Price escalation", "Express warranty"],
 "Making time of the essence means a missed deadline can be a material breach. A no-damage-for-delay clause limits a contractor to extra time with no money for delays."),
]
